module Darklang.Cli.Docs.HttpServer

let content(): String =
  """# HTTP Server

## Start
  serve MyApp.router                        # --port defaults to 8080
  serve MyApp.router --port 9090 --max-body-bytes 1048576
  serve MyApp.router --live                 # follows your edits; an open page reloads
  # `http-server` is an alias for `serve`. Flags may come in any order.

The argument is the fully-qualified name of a function in the package tree, not a
file. It must be `Stdlib.Http.Request -> Stdlib.Http.Response`.

With `--live`, every request runs the newest router that passes its checks, and
a broken save keeps the previous one serving. Without it the version resolved at
start serves until you restart. Each request is its own
process: a slow handler holds up nobody; one past `http.requestTimeoutMs` (30 s
unset) is cancelled and answers 504; one that raises answers 500 with its error;
one stopped from `ps cancel` answers 503. `docs live` has the rest.

## A router

Handlers are values; `routeRequest` dispatches to them by method and path.

  module MyApp

  let helloFn(req: Stdlib.Http.Request): Stdlib.Http.Response =
    Stdlib.Http.responseWithText(200, "Hello, World!")

  val hello =
    Stdlib.HttpServer.Handler
      { route = "/hello"
        method = "GET"
        handler = helloFn }

  val handlers = [ hello ]

  // This is what you pass to `serve`. It has to be a named function.
  let router(req: Stdlib.Http.Request): Stdlib.Http.Response =
    Stdlib.HttpServer.routeRequest(handlers, req)

## Request

  type Stdlib.Http.Request =
    { method: String, url: String, headers: List<String * String>, body: Bytes }

  req.method                            // "GET", "POST", ...

The path is not a field - it's derived from the url:

  Stdlib.HttpServer.getPath(req)        // "/users/42"
  Stdlib.Http.parseQueryString(req.url)  // query string -> Dict

`body` is a Bytes. For text or JSON, convert first:

  let text = Stdlib.Utf8.decodeWithReplacement(req.body)

## Response

Status first, then the body. Headers, when you give them, go in between.

  Stdlib.Http.responseWithText(200, "Hello")
  Stdlib.Http.responseWithHtml(200, "<h1>Hi</h1>")
  Stdlib.Http.responseWithJson(200, jsonString)
  Stdlib.Http.responseWithHeaders(200, [ ("x-thing", "value") ], bodyBytes)
  Stdlib.Http.response(200, bodyBytes)  // the raw one; body is a Bytes
  Stdlib.Http.notFound()

A status is a plain number here. `Stdlib.Http.Status` names the common ones and
checks the rest: `Status.toInt64(Status.tooManyRequests)` is 429, and
`Status.fromInt64(n)` is an Error outside 100 to 599.

## Route params

  // given route "/user/:id" and a request for "/user/42"
  match Stdlib.HttpServer.getPathParam(req, "/user/:id", "id") with
  | Some(id) -> Stdlib.Http.responseWithText(200, $"user {id}")
  | None -> Stdlib.Http.notFound()

`matchRoute pattern path` is the lower-level form. It returns a record, not an
Option: `{ matched: Bool, vars: List<String * String> }`.

## Testing a router without a server

`request` calls the router once, in-process, as a default `serve` would, and
prints the status, headers and body. No port, no bind permission, no background
process:

  request MyApp.router GET /hello
  request MyApp.router POST /items --header content-type:application/json --body -

A test calls it the same way, with `Stdlib.Http.Request.make`:

  test hello =
    let req = Stdlib.Http.Request.make("GET", "/hello", [], Stdlib.Bytes.empty)
    MyApp.router(req).statusCode |> Stdlib.Test.equal(200)

## Permissions and file storage

Grant the bind address and port before starting the server:

  permissions allow http-server 127.0.0.1 9090
  serve MyApp.router --port 9090

If a handler stores data in a file, grant access to its directory:

  permissions allow file read <dir>
  permissions allow file write <dir>

Check for a missing file separately from other read errors before treating it as empty.

## Middleware

A handler is `Stdlib.Http.Request -> Stdlib.Http.Response`, so middleware is a
function from a handler to a handler:

  type Stdlib.HttpServer.Middleware =
    (Http.Request -> Http.Response) -> (Http.Request -> Http.Response)

`serve` runs every request through `config.middleware`, first listed
outermost. `Config.defaults(port)` gives `Middleware.defaults()`:

  Stdlib.HttpServer.Middleware.logRequests()      // [HttpServer] GET /a 200 3ms, on stderr
  Stdlib.HttpServer.Middleware.standardHeaders()  // HSTS

The log line, and the server's own line and telemetry, leave the query out,
since it may carry a token. Not in the defaults:

  Stdlib.HttpServer.Middleware.cors([ "https://app.example.com" ])  // or [ "*" ]
  Stdlib.HttpServer.Middleware.requestId()        // X-Request-Id, the client's or a new one

`cors` answers a browser's preflight from a listed origin with 204, and adds
`Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to everything else it sends one.

`Server: darklang` is on every response whatever the list. Behind a proxy that
sets `X-Forwarded-Proto`, add `Middleware.trustForwardedProto()` so the handler
sees the `https://` url the client used; it is not a default, since any client
can send that header.

Change the list with a record update:

  let config =
    { Stdlib.HttpServer.Config.defaults(port) with
        middleware =
          [ Stdlib.HttpServer.Middleware.logRequests()
            Stdlib.HttpServer.Middleware.onResponse(fun r ->
              { r with headers = Stdlib.List.append(r.headers, ("X-Frame-Options", "DENY")) }) ] }

`Middleware.onRequest(f)` and `Middleware.onResponse(f)` build the common two;
`Middleware.wrap(ms, handler)` is the composition, if you want it yourself.
The router reaches the server bare and is wrapped per request, so its
approvals and its traces stay its own. The middleware runs under the server's
grants, and `serve` checks what it can reach before it binds: a log line needs
`clock` and `stdout`.

Compression, the body limit and the handler timeout are not middleware, and
neither are the responses the server writes itself (a 413, a handler that
failed or timed out). Each gets a log line whatever the list holds, and HSTS
when `standardHeaders` is in it, as every other response does. A handler that
fails prints its error.

## A worked example

`Darklang.DemoData.HttpServerTest` is a small server that runs - root, /hello and
a POST /echo. Read it with `view Darklang.DemoData.HttpServerTest.router`.
"""
